WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.710
Hey everyone, welcome back to another explainer.

2
00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:04.403
We are going zero fluff today.

3
00:00:04.403 --> 00:00:13.210
This one is designed specifically for those of you gearing up for your CFP final exams and we are laser-focused on health insurance and government programs.

4
00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:21.453
My goal today is super simple: help you lock in those crucial points and dodge the absolute trickiest traps the test writers love to throw at you.

5
00:00:21.453 --> 00:00:28.961
So, if you already know the basics, but really want to solidify the most heavily tested concepts, you are in exactly the right place.

6
00:00:28.961 --> 00:00:30.655
Here is our roadmap for today.

7
00:00:30.655 --> 00:00:37.768
We're hitting private plans and HSAs, COBRA rules, Medicare parts A through D, the notorious long-term care trap, and finally,

8
00:00:37.768 --> 00:00:39.349
your top test day takeaways.

9
00:00:39.349 --> 00:00:40.704
Let's get right into it.

10
00:00:40.704 --> 00:00:42.736
Section one, private plans and HSAs.

11
00:00:42.736 --> 00:00:49.567
We're kicking things off with working-age private plans and the foundational structures you really just have to memorize.

12
00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:52.728
Okay, so let's look at the big three managed care plans.

13
00:00:52.728 --> 00:00:54.930
You've got to know the difference here.

14
00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.003
Think about an HMO.

15
00:00:56.003 --> 00:00:57.301
It's highly restricted.

16
00:00:57.301 --> 00:01:02.664
You're dealing with capitation fees and that mandatory gatekeeper, your primary care physician.

17
00:01:02.664 --> 00:01:10.399
If your client wants to see a specific out-of-network specialist, they can't just call them up; they absolutely need that referral first.

18
00:01:10.399 --> 00:01:18.076
And if the exam mentions a fixed monthly payment to a medical provider, regardless of how many times the patient actually goes in, boom,

19
00:01:18.076 --> 00:01:19.431
that's a capitation fee.

20
00:01:19.431 --> 00:01:20.334
HMO all the way.

21
00:01:20.334 --> 00:01:22.028
Now, contrast that with a PPO.

22
00:01:22.028 --> 00:01:24.512
This is your flexible fee-for-service model.

23
00:01:24.512 --> 00:01:30.214
You get discounted rates in-network, but you have the total freedom to go out-of-network if you want.

24
00:01:30.214 --> 00:01:33.545
And don't let the POS or point of service plan trip you up.

25
00:01:33.545 --> 00:01:35.013
It's really just a hybrid.

26
00:01:35.013 --> 00:01:39.021
It leans on a network, but usually still relies on that PCP gatekeeper.

27
00:01:39.021 --> 00:01:45.118
Now, before we can even breathe the word HSA, we have to talk about the high deductible health plan or HDHP.

28
00:01:45.118 --> 00:01:46.981
The exam loves testing this link.

29
00:01:46.981 --> 00:01:49.747
You literally cannot have an HSA without an HDHP.

30
00:01:49.747 --> 00:01:53.812
With an HDHP, your client gets the benefit of nice low monthly premiums,

31
00:01:53.812 --> 00:02:01.490
but the trade-off: they have to meet a seriously high deductible out-of-pocket before the insurance actually kicks in and starts paying.

32
00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:06.401
And that brings us to the HSA, which is honestly basically a superhero of the tax code.

33
00:02:06.401 --> 00:02:07.982
It is triple tax advantaged.

34
00:02:07.982 --> 00:02:09.901
You absolutely must memorize this.

35
00:02:09.901 --> 00:02:12.272
First, contributions go in tax-deductible.

36
00:02:12.272 --> 00:02:15.321
Second, earnings inside the account grow tax-deferred.

37
00:02:15.321 --> 00:02:20.176
Third, distributions are 100% tax-free if you use them for qualified medical expenses.

38
00:02:20.176 --> 00:02:25.821
Plus, thanks to the CARES Act, you can even use it for over-the-counter meds without a prescription.

39
00:02:25.821 --> 00:02:26.047
Now,

40
00:02:26.047 --> 00:02:33.838
a quick pro-tip for your specific exam window: make sure you memorize the exact current year contribution limits for singles and families.

41
00:02:33.838 --> 00:02:40.838
And here is a massive trap to watch out for: if a scenario says that client has a low deductible or traditional health plan,

42
00:02:40.838 --> 00:02:43.774
they are completely disqualified from having an HSA.

43
00:02:43.774 --> 00:02:44.677
Zero exceptions.

44
00:02:44.677 --> 00:02:46.879
Section two, COBRA rules and timelines.

45
00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:52.411
Let's look at what happens when someone loses their job and those strict numbers you need to know.

46
00:02:52.411 --> 00:02:56.137
All right, pay super close attention to the 20 employee threshold.

47
00:02:56.137 --> 00:03:02.122
COBRA mandates that employers let former employees and their dependents keep their group health insurance.

48
00:03:02.122 --> 00:03:06.864
But, and this is a huge but, it only applies to companies with 20 or more employees.

49
00:03:06.864 --> 00:03:12.340
So, if the exam gives you this beautifully written heart-wrenching story about a laid-off worker,

50
00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:16.743
but casually mentions the company only has 15 employees, COBRA does not apply.

51
00:03:16.743 --> 00:03:20.582
Do not let the emotional story distract you from that simple number.

52
00:03:20.582 --> 00:03:23.857
Duration is exactly where they'll try to trip you up next.

53
00:03:23.857 --> 00:03:29.954
For a normal termination or a voluntary reduction in hours, standard COBRA coverage lasts exactly 18 months.

54
00:03:29.954 --> 00:03:32.042
Lock that 18 in your brain right now.

55
00:03:32.042 --> 00:03:34.639
The test might try to bait you with 36 months,

56
00:03:34.639 --> 00:03:40.680
but remember that's only for dependents and secondary events like a divorce or if the employee passes away.

57
00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.938
Okay, for standard termination, it's 18.

58
00:03:42.938 --> 00:03:46.269
Oh, and if they're fired for gross misconduct, zero months.

59
00:03:46.269 --> 00:03:47.511
So, who pays for this?

60
00:03:47.511 --> 00:03:51.180
Here's the cost trap: this coverage is definitely not subsidized.

61
00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:54.681
The terminated employee is on the hook for the entire premium.

62
00:03:54.681 --> 00:03:56.600
The employer pays absolutely zero.

63
00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:03.431
Plus, the employee gets hit with a 2% administrative fee, bringing their total out-of-pocket cost to 102% of the premium.

64
00:04:03.431 --> 00:04:05.746
Keep that 102% number handy for test day.

65
00:04:05.746 --> 00:04:08.851
Section three, demystifying Medicare parts A through D.

66
00:04:08.851 --> 00:04:12.181
Time to jump into retirement and decode this alphabet soup.

67
00:04:12.181 --> 00:04:14.609
Original Medicare basically has two halves.

68
00:04:14.609 --> 00:04:18.899
Part A handles your inpatient hospital stays, hospice, and some home health.

69
00:04:18.899 --> 00:04:27.876
The big takeaway for the test: it's usually totally premium-free as long as the client or their spouse worked and paid Medicare taxes for at least 40 quarters,

70
00:04:27.876 --> 00:04:29.513
which translates to 10 years.

71
00:04:29.513 --> 00:04:35.610
Part B, on the other hand, is for outpatient stuff, seeing the doctor, getting lab tests, and unlike Part A,

72
00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:37.473
Part B charges a monthly premium.

73
00:04:37.473 --> 00:04:40.465
Oh, and definitely watch out for the IRMAA surcharge.

74
00:04:40.465 --> 00:04:43.683
If your client has a high modified adjusted gross income,

75
00:04:43.683 --> 00:04:49.046
they'll have to pay a tested income-related surcharge on both their Part B and Part D premiums.

76
00:04:49.046 --> 00:04:51.530
Now, whatever you do, do not mix up C and D.

77
00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:53.167
Part C is Medicare Advantage.

78
00:04:53.167 --> 00:04:59.151
Think of it as a managed care plan offered by private insurers that completely replaces original Medicare.

79
00:04:59.151 --> 00:05:04.684
It bundles A and B together and usually throws in vision, dental, and drugs into one neat package.

80
00:05:04.684 --> 00:05:08.128
Part D is specifically standalone prescription drug coverage.

81
00:05:08.128 --> 00:05:14.451
Once the client hits their deductible on Part D, they usually face a 25% cost share until they hit their limits.

82
00:05:14.451 --> 00:05:19.757
A really good trick to remember this: D is for drugs; C is for comprehensive advantage bundle.

83
00:05:19.757 --> 00:05:25.459
Now, what if they don't want an advantage plan and just want to stick with original Medicare A and B?

84
00:05:25.459 --> 00:05:29.129
That's where Medigap or Medicare supplement plans come into play.

85
00:05:29.129 --> 00:05:34.492
Private companies offer these 10 standard plans labeled A through N to literally fill the gaps,

86
00:05:34.492 --> 00:05:38.331
paying the deductibles and co-insurance that Medicare leaves behind.

87
00:05:38.331 --> 00:05:45.783
But here is a massive warning: Medigap policies absolutely do not cover long-term care needs like custodial care or assisted living.

88
00:05:45.783 --> 00:05:47.928
Section four, the long-term care trap.

89
00:05:47.928 --> 00:05:51.315
This is literally where so many candidates lose easy points.

90
00:05:51.315 --> 00:05:52.557
So, let's clear it up.

91
00:05:52.557 --> 00:05:57.751
Picture this classic exam question: you've got an 80-year-old client with cognitive decline.

92
00:05:57.751 --> 00:06:05.542
They need to move into a nursing home for daily help with activities of daily living, the ADLs, things like eating, bathing, and dressing.

93
00:06:05.542 --> 00:06:08.364
That is the textbook definition of custodial care.

94
00:06:08.364 --> 00:06:12.373
So, the exam asks, will Medicare foot the bill for this long-term stay?

95
00:06:12.373 --> 00:06:14.349
The answer is a giant screaming no.

96
00:06:14.349 --> 00:06:16.042
Seriously, do not fall for it.

97
00:06:16.042 --> 00:06:19.373
You need to permanently draw a line in the sand right here.

98
00:06:19.373 --> 00:06:25.752
Medicare is strictly for short-term medically necessary skilled nursing care following a qualified hospital stay.

99
00:06:25.752 --> 00:06:27.502
It's meant to rehabilitate you.

100
00:06:27.502 --> 00:06:30.495
It literally never pays for long-term custodial care.

101
00:06:30.495 --> 00:06:38.172
Medicaid, on the other hand, is the joint federal and state program that actually does cover long-term custodial nursing home care, but,

102
00:06:38.172 --> 00:06:41.560
and this is huge, only for individuals who are impoverished.

103
00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:49.124
Because Medicaid is a safety net for the impoverished, clients who have significant assets have to spend down their wealth to qualify.

104
00:06:49.124 --> 00:06:52.681
And you better believe the exam will test the look-back period.

105
00:06:52.681 --> 00:06:58.778
There's a brutal 60-month look-back penalty for hiding or transferring money to heirs just to get qualified.

106
00:06:58.778 --> 00:07:05.214
Imagine a client tries to basically gift their vacation home to their kids to look poor on paper and get Medicaid.

107
00:07:05.214 --> 00:07:10.859
If they do that within 5 years of applying, they're going to get hit with severe eligibility delays.

108
00:07:10.859 --> 00:07:17.747
Now, in calculating what needs to be spent down, a few things are actually exempt: usually the primary residence, one car,

109
00:07:17.747 --> 00:07:21.868
personal property, and life insurance if the face value is under $1, 500.

110
00:07:21.868 --> 00:07:25.594
Everything else has to be spent on care before Medicaid swoops in.

111
00:07:25.594 --> 00:07:27.739
Section five, top five test takeaways.

112
00:07:27.739 --> 00:07:32.594
Let's wrap this whole thing up with the absolute highest probability facts you'll see.

113
00:07:32.594 --> 00:07:38.691
If you remember literally nothing else from this explainer today, burn these five concepts into your memory.

114
00:07:38.691 --> 00:07:42.530
First, you cannot have an HSA without a high deductible health plan.

115
00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:42.925
Second,

116
00:07:42.925 --> 00:07:50.490
COBRA only applies to companies with 20 or more employees; the standard duration is 18 months and the client pays 102% of the premium.

117
00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:54.781
Third, Medicare Part C is the private advantage bundle; Part D is for drugs.

118
00:07:54.781 --> 00:07:59.297
Fourth, the trap of all traps: Medicare does not cover long-term custodial care.

119
00:07:59.297 --> 00:07:59.692
Period.

120
00:07:59.692 --> 00:08:06.523
Fifth, Medicaid does cover long-term care, but it requires impoverishment and enforces a harsh 60-month look-back period.

121
00:08:06.523 --> 00:08:08.725
The test writers are incredibly sneaky.

122
00:08:08.725 --> 00:08:15.782
They're going to try to trick you with a 15-employee COBRA question or a really complicated Medicare long-term care scenario.

123
00:08:15.782 --> 00:08:21.766
But honestly, with these structural distinctions locked down in your mind, you're totally equipped for it.

124
00:08:21.766 --> 00:08:24.363
The real question is, once you pass this exam,

125
00:08:24.363 --> 00:08:29.895
how will you use these exact rules to save your future clients thousands of dollars in retirement?

126
00:08:29.895 --> 00:08:31.758
Keep that broader impact in mind.

127
00:08:31.758 --> 00:08:34.694
Stay focused and I'll see you in the next explainer.

